I’m sorry, I didn’t provide enough details earlier, which might have caused some misunderstanding.
```cpp
        // Skip this case to avoid false errors caused by regex '$' anchors
        auto firstCharIt = std::next(findStart.begin());
        if (firstCharIt != strPtr->end() && *firstCharIt == '\'')
        {
            lg2::info("String value: `{STR}`", "STR", *strPtr);
            break;
        }
```
output:
```log
<6> String value: `xyz.openbmc_project.Inventory.Source.DevicePresence({'Name': '^RPU1LeakPort1$'})`
```

The case I mentioned isn’t about having a dollar sign in plain text like `Some$ random thing`, but rather about regex expressions such as `xyz.openbmc_project.Inventory.Source.DevicePresence({'Name': '^RPU1LeakPort1$'})`, where `$` is a valid end-of-line anchor. 
Those were being falsely reported as leftover template variables.

> If there is any mistake in the config file, it is the user's fault and the user can fix their config file.
> How would we detect if the $ is there by mistake or on purpose?

Actually, I have the same question. If we simply skip every `$`, we might also miss real cases where the user accidentally left a `$` by mistake.
That’s why I only handled the specific `$'` case.

The logic I meant is:

```
        if (findStart.end() == strPtr->end())
        {
            lg2::info("Skipping regex $ anchor");
            break;
        }
```

If "$" is at the end of the JSON value string, then end() of findStart will be end() strPtr.
